T={
"sglt2":[("SGLT2 inhibitors act on the:",["Early proximal tubule","Thick ascending limb","Distal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct"],0,"Block glucose and Na⁺ reabsorption → glucosuria and mild natriuresis."),
 ("SGLT2 inhibitors protect the kidney in CKD mainly by:",["Restoring tubuloglomerular feedback","Dilating the afferent arteriole","Blocking aldosterone receptors","Raising GFR through hyperfiltration"],0,"More Na⁺ at the macula densa → afferent constriction → ↓ intraglomerular pressure; an initial small eGFR dip is expected."),
 ('A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes (HbA1c 8.1%), eGFR 55 mL/min/1.73 m² and albuminuria starts a new oral drug. Two weeks later urine dipstick shows 3+ glucose with a plasma glucose of 130 mg/dL, and he develops a genital yeast infection. The drug acts by inhibiting which transporter?', ['Na⁺–glucose cotransporter in the early PT', 'Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ cotransporter in the TAL', 'Na⁺–Cl⁻ cotransporter in the early DCT', 'Epithelial Na⁺ channel in collecting duct'], 0, 'Glucosuria at normal plasma glucose plus mycotic genital infection points to an SGLT2 inhibitor (e.g., empagliflozin) blocking proximal Na⁺–glucose reabsorption; NKCC2 is the loop diuretic target.', 3)],
"adh":[("Tolvaptan is used for:",["Hyponatremia due to SIADH","Central diabetes insipidus","Hypernatremia from water loss","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus"],0,"V₂ antagonist for euvolemic or hypervolemic hyponatremia (SIADH, HF); also slows ADPKD; hepatotoxic."),
 ("Desmopressin treats central DI by acting on:",["V₂ receptors in the collecting duct","V₁ receptors on vascular smooth muscle","Mineralocorticoid receptors","NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb"],0,"V₂ → AQP2 insertion. Nephrogenic DI does not respond; use thiazide and amiloride (for lithium)."),
 ('A 62-year-old man with small cell lung cancer has confusion. He is euvolemic; serum Na⁺ 124 mEq/L, serum osmolality 258 mOsm/kg, urine osmolality 480 mOsm/kg, urine Na⁺ 45 mEq/L. Fluid restriction fails. Which drug directly targets the cause of his hyponatremia?', ['Tolvaptan', 'Desmopressin', 'Fludrocortisone', 'Hydrochlorothiazide'], 0, 'Ectopic ADH (SIADH) is countered by the V₂ receptor antagonist tolvaptan; desmopressin is a V₂ agonist and would worsen it.', 3)],
"resist":[("Diuretic resistance in heart failure can be overcome by adding:",["A thiazide (e.g., metolazone)","Oral potassium supplements","An ACE inhibitor (enalapril)","An osmotic diuretic (mannitol)"],0,"Sequential nephron blockade: metolazone + loop; watch K⁺ and Na⁺."),
 ("A cause of loop diuretic resistance:",["Distal tubule hypertrophy","Concurrent hypokalemia","Low dietary salt intake","Elevated serum uric acid"],0,"Hypertrophied distal tubule ↑ Na⁺ reabsorption; also NSAIDs, poor absorption (gut edema), low renal perfusion."),
 ('A 71-year-old woman with heart failure remains edematous despite IV furosemide 80 mg twice daily; urine output is low and spot urine Na⁺ is 25 mEq/L. Metolazone is added and diuresis improves. Metolazone works here by blocking:', ['NCC in the hypertrophied distal tubule', 'NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb', 'ENaC in collecting duct principal cells', 'Carbonic anhydrase in proximal tubule'], 0, 'Chronic loop diuretic use causes distal tubule hypertrophy with ↑ Na⁺ reabsorption; a thiazide-like drug blocks NCC there (sequential nephron blockade). NKCC2 is already blocked by furosemide.', 3)],
"aki":[("Which drug combination raises AKI risk ('triple whammy')?",["NSAID + ACEi/ARB + diuretic","Statin + aspirin + ezetimibe","PPI + metformin + insulin","β-blocker + CCB + nitrate"],0,"Afferent constriction, efferent dilation and volume depletion."),
 ("Aminoglycoside nephrotoxicity typically causes:",["Nonoliguric ATN after 5–7 days","Immediate anuria after one dose","Nephrotic-range proteinuria","Renal papillary necrosis"],0,"Proximal tubular injury; monitor levels and creatinine."),
 ('A 72-year-old man on lisinopril and hydrochlorothiazide starts ibuprofen for knee osteoarthritis. After 3 days of diarrhea his creatinine rises from 1.0 to 2.6 mg/dL; urine sediment is bland and FENa is 0.5%. How does ibuprofen contribute to this injury?', ['↓ Prostaglandin-mediated afferent dilation', '↓ Angiotensin II-mediated efferent tone', 'Direct necrosis of proximal tubule cells', 'Allergic tubulointerstitial inflammation'], 0, "Prerenal AKI: NSAIDs remove prostaglandin afferent vasodilation while the ACE inhibitor removes efferent constriction ('triple whammy'). Loss of efferent tone is the ACE inhibitor's contribution, not the NSAID's.", 3)],
"ckd":[("Drugs proven to slow proteinuric CKD:",["ACEi/ARB plus SGLT2 inhibitors","Loop diuretics such as furosemide","Allopurinol for all patients","Dihydropyridine CCBs alone"],0,"± finerenone in diabetes; all lower intraglomerular pressure or fibrosis."),
 ("After starting an ACE inhibitor, a creatinine rise up to about 30% is:",["Expected and acceptable","An indication to stop at once","A sign of acute tubular necrosis","Usually due to dehydration"],0,"Reflects efferent dilation; stop if higher or K⁺ rises dangerously."),
 ('A 55-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes has BP 142/88 mm Hg, eGFR 48 mL/min/1.73 m² and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio 600 mg/g. Lisinopril is started. Its main renoprotective mechanism is:', ['Efferent dilation lowering glomerular pressure', 'Afferent constriction lowering renal blood flow', 'Restoring nephrin in the podocyte slit diaphragm', 'Increasing tubular reabsorption of albumin'], 0, 'Blocking angiotensin II dilates the efferent arteriole, reducing intraglomerular pressure and albuminuria in diabetic nephropathy; it does not constrict the afferent arteriole.', 3)],
"anemia":[("Before starting an ESA in CKD anemia, ensure:",["Adequate iron stores","A high baseline hemoglobin","Low vitamin D levels","A normal PTH level"],0,"Check ferritin and TSAT; iron deficiency causes ESA hyporesponsiveness."),
 ("Targeting a high hemoglobin (> 13 g/dL) with ESAs increases:",["Stroke, thrombosis and CV events","Long-term overall survival","Intestinal iron absorption","Bone mineral density"],0,"Keep Hb around 10–11.5 g/dL."),
 ('A 64-year-old man with CKD stage 4 (eGFR 22 mL/min/1.73 m²) has fatigue. Hb 8.9 g/dL, MCV 88 fL, low reticulocyte count, ferritin 450 ng/mL, transferrin saturation 30%; stool occult blood is negative. Most appropriate treatment?', ['Darbepoetin alfa', 'Oral ferrous sulfate', 'IV iron sucrose', 'Folic acid'], 0, 'Normocytic hypoproliferative anemia of CKD reflects ↓ renal erythropoietin; with iron stores already adequate an ESA is indicated. Iron would help only if ferritin/TSAT were low.', 3)],
"mbd":[("Calcitriol is used in CKD because:",["1α-hydroxylation is impaired","It lowers serum phosphate","It lowers serum calcium levels","It blocks PTH receptors on bone"],0,"Renal 1α-hydroxylase is lost; calcitriol raises calcium and suppresses PTH; can raise phosphate."),
 ("A calcium-free phosphate binder:",["Sevelamer or lanthanum","Calcium carbonate","Calcium acetate","Aluminum hydroxide long term"],0,"Avoids calcium loading and vascular calcification."),
 ('A 60-year-old woman on hemodialysis has pruritus. Serum Ca²⁺ 9.8 mg/dL, phosphate 7.2 mg/dL, PTH 650 pg/mL; CT shows extensive coronary artery calcification. Dietary phosphate restriction has failed. Best drug to lower her phosphate?', ['Sevelamer', 'Calcium acetate', 'Calcitriol', 'Aluminum hydroxide'], 0, 'A non-calcium binder avoids adding calcium load to existing vascular calcification; calcitriol raises gut phosphate absorption and aluminum is toxic long term.', 2)],
"hyperk":[("In hyperkalemia with ECG changes, the first drug is:",["IV calcium gluconate","IV regular insulin","IV furosemide bolus","Oral patiromer (binder)"],0,"Stabilizes the cardiac membrane; does not lower K⁺."),
 ("Which drugs shift K⁺ into cells?",["Insulin and β₂ agonists","IV calcium gluconate","Patiromer (K⁺ binder)","Spironolactone"],0,"Insulin is given with glucose. Removal: diuretics, binders (patiromer, SZC), dialysis."),
 ('A 66-year-old man with end-stage renal disease missed two dialysis sessions and has muscle weakness. Serum K⁺ is 7.4 mEq/L and the ECG shows peaked T waves and a widened QRS. The first drug given acts by:', ['Stabilizing the cardiac myocyte membrane', 'Shifting K⁺ into cells via Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase', 'Binding K⁺ in the colon for excretion', 'Increasing K⁺ secretion in collecting duct'], 0, 'IV calcium gluconate antagonizes the membrane effects of hyperkalemia within minutes but does not lower K⁺; insulin/β₂ agonists shift K⁺ in afterwards.', 3)],
"sodium":[("Rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia risks:",["Osmotic demyelination syndrome","Worsening cerebral edema","Acute hyperkalemic arrhythmia","Seizures from persistent low Na⁺"],0,"Limit correction to about 8–10 mEq/L in 24 h."),
 ("Which diuretic class is a common cause of hyponatremia?",["Thiazides","Loop diuretics","Osmotic diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"],0,"Impair urinary dilution in the DCT."),
 ('A 45-year-old woman with alcohol use disorder is admitted with serum Na⁺ 108 mEq/L; after hypertonic saline her Na⁺ is 126 mEq/L 24 hours later. Three days later she develops dysarthria, dysphagia and quadriparesis, and MRI shows a T2 lesion in the central pons. Most likely diagnosis?', ['Osmotic demyelination syndrome', 'Wernicke encephalopathy', 'Brainstem ischemic stroke', 'Guillain–Barré syndrome'], 0, 'An 18 mEq/L rise in 24 h exceeds the safe limit (~8–10) and causes central pontine myelinolysis; Wernicke causes ophthalmoplegia, ataxia and confusion, not a pontine lesion.', 2)],
"acidbase":[("Acetazolamide causes which acid–base disturbance?",["Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis","Acute respiratory acidosis","High anion gap metabolic acidosis"],0,"Hyperchloremic acidosis from urinary HCO₃⁻ loss."),
 ("Loop and thiazide diuretics cause:",["Hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis","Hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis","Hyperkalemic metabolic acidosis","Primary respiratory alkalosis"],0,"Contraction alkalosis plus ↑ distal H⁺ and K⁺ secretion."),
 ('A 28-year-old man takes a drug for altitude sickness prophylaxis before a trek. Labs: pH 7.32, HCO₃⁻ 17 mEq/L, PaCO₂ 32 mm Hg, Na⁺ 140 mEq/L, Cl⁻ 113 mEq/L; urine pH 7.5. His acid–base disturbance results from:', ['Proximal HCO₃⁻ wasting from CA inhibition', 'Failure of distal H⁺ secretion', 'Accumulation of unmeasured organic anions', 'Alveolar hyperventilation alone'], 0, 'Acetazolamide blocks proximal carbonic anhydrase → urinary HCO₃⁻ loss and a normal anion gap (10) metabolic acidosis with alkaline urine; organic anions would raise the gap.', 3)],
}
